## Using colour combinations on maps

- Use as few colours as the map needs. Every extra colour is one more thing to look up in the key.
- Use one data colour, Accent green with a Primary green outline, for zones and markers. Tell them apart by shape, letter and label rather than by a second colour.
- Give data on the map, such as a zone or a marker, at least 3:1 contrast with the base map. If it falls short, add an outline, simplify the map or give the same facts in a table.
- Give every map a key that names each colour, shape and line style.
- Do not let the colour of an area suggest a political party.
- Test the map with the people who will use it.

[Test combinations and keep another cue](colour.html#bg-col-03) explains the contrast that text and controls need.

### Example: zones on a base map

Three Home Assistant zones, Home, Work and School, drawn on a base map. Notice that every zone uses the same colour with a darker outline, and that each zone is named, so you can tell them apart without colour.

<%= colour_example('map-govuk-zones', layout: :map, callout: false) %>

### Colours used in the example

#### Points of interest

Zones and markers are Accent green with a Primary green outline. Accent green on its own is too pale against the land, so the outline gives the 3:1 contrast. Labels, such as zone names, are Black.

<%= colour_swatches(%w[green-accent green black], key: 'example') %>

#### Base map

Land is Black tint 95% with a Black tint 80% outline. Sea is Teal tint 95%, with a band of Teal tint 80% along the coast. On the working example below, roads are White.

<%= colour_swatches(%w[white black-tint-80 black-tint-95 teal-tint-80 teal-tint-95], key: 'example') %>

## Working OpenStreetMap example

The same colours on real OpenStreetMap data around Cardiff Bay, with 2 device markers. Nothing is requested from OpenStreetMap until you select **Load OpenStreetMap**.

When the map loads, notice that:

- roads, such as Bute Street and Bute Place, are White with Black tint 80% edges
- buildings are Black tint 80% on Black tint 95% land
- the water of Roath Basin is Teal tint 95%, with a band of Teal tint 80% along its edge
- markers A and B are both Accent green with a Primary green outline, and their shapes and letters tell them apart

Rivers and streams are thin Teal tint 80% lines, which have low contrast with the land, so do not rely on them to show where something is.

<%= example('map-neighbourhood-records') %>

[Colours used in the example](#colours-used-in-the-example) has swatches of every colour on this map.

## In Home Assistant

civica ships no restyle of the native Map card, which keeps its own map styles. You can give the base map these colours yourself by setting a custom `map_style` with `colors` and `colors_dark`: [What the native Map card offers](../home-assistant/maps-and-zones.html#what-the-native-map-card-offers) explains the options. Use the same colours for both, because map colours do not change in dark mode.

The `colors` setting covers only the base map, such as land, water, roads, buildings and boundaries. The Map card colours zones and markers from the theme instead. The Home zone takes the primary colour, which follows your theme colour and light or dark mode. A passive zone takes the secondary text colour. Other zones, and people and device trackers, take the numbered colours in the order they were added to Home Assistant, which civica sets to its chart colours. So Accent green zones and markers apply only to maps you draw yourself.

Checked on 18 November 2026 against the Home Assistant frontend source at commit `b1164739c3a853a28ba1c425415e96a17eb6286e`.

